+\section{Arithmetic currents and prime inclusions}
+\label{sec:wc-currents}
+
+The prime forms define creation and annihilation currents with the native
+convolution involution.  Orthogonal prime coordinates give compatible
+inclusions for all finite sets of primes.  Their Hilbert sum also supports
+infinite families of currents with square-summable prime coordinates.
+The arithmetic diagonal does not belong to this infinite Hilbert sum.
+
+All vector spaces are complex.  Inner products are linear in their first
+argument.  Algebra elements have degree zero.  Particle number gives a
+separate nonnegative grading, with ordinary commutators throughout.
+No coefficient change, deformation parameter, or zero spectrum enters
+the construction.
+
+For a prime $p$, retain every power $p^m$ and set
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ c_{p,m}&=(\log p)p^{-m/2},&
+ C_p&=\sum_{m\geq1}c_{p,m}=\frac{\log p}{\sqrt p-1},\\
+ \lambda_p(t)&=\sum_{m\geq1}c_{p,m}|1-e^{itm\log p}|^2,&
+ d\mu_p(t)&=\lambda_p(t)\frac{dt}{2\pi},\\
+ W_p(g)&=\sum_{m\geq1}c_{p,m}
+ \bigl(2g(0)-g(m\log p)-g(-m\log p)\bigr).
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-local-data}
+\end{equation}
+The density is given explicitly by \eqref{eq:wp-euler-density}.
+In particular, $0\leq\lambda_p\leq4C_p$ and $\lambda_p>0$ almost
+everywhere.  The measure is locally finite and has infinite total mass.
+The Fourier map of \eqref{eq:fourier} gives
+\[
+ H_p=L^2(\R,\mu_p),\qquad
+ T_p f=\widehat f,\qquad
+ \langle T_pf,T_ph\rangle=W_p(h^*\star f).
+\]
+Theorem~\ref{thm:wp-carrier} proves that $T_p(\cA_\zeta)$ is dense in $H_p$.
+
+\subsection{The two-prime calculation}
+
+Begin with $H_{\{2,3\}}=H_2\oplus H_3$ and
+$v_2(f)=(T_2f,0)$, $v_3(f)=(0,T_3f)$.
+Their inner products are
+\begin{equation}
+ \langle v_p(f),v_q(h)\rangle
+ =\delta_{pq}W_p(h^*\star f),\qquad p,q\in\{2,3\}.
+ \label{eq:wc-two-prime-covariance}
+\end{equation}
+Thus $v_2(f)+v_3(f)$ realizes the sum of both full local Euler factors.
+For $f=g_a$, where
+$g_a(u)=(4\pi a)^{-1/2}e^{-u^2/(4a)}$ and $a>0$, its squared norm is
+\begin{equation}
+ q_2(a)+q_3(a),\qquad
+ q_p(a)=\frac{\log p}{\sqrt{2\pi a}}
+ \sum_{m\geq1}p^{-m/2}
+ \left(1-e^{-m^2(\log p)^2/(8a)}\right)>0.
+ \label{eq:wc-gaussian-norm}
+\end{equation}
+This follows from $g_a^*\star g_a=g_{2a}$ and the displayed value of
+$W_p(g_{2a})$.  Truncation after $M$ powers has error at most
+\[
+ \frac{\log p}{\sqrt{2\pi a}}
+ \frac{p^{-(M+1)/2}}{1-p^{-1/2}}.
+\]
+
+Let $\Omega$ have norm one in particle number zero.
+The current formulas below give, for $p,q\in\{2,3\}$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ A_p^+(f)\Omega&=v_p(f),& A_p^-(f)\Omega&=0,\\
+ A_p^-(f)A_q^+(h)\Omega
+ &=\delta_{pq}W_p(f\star h)\Omega,&
+ A_p^+(f)A_q^+(h)\Omega
+ &=\sqrt2\,S_2(v_p(f)\otimes v_q(h)).
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-two-prime-action}
+\end{equation}
+Here $S_2(x\otimes y)=(x\otimes y+y\otimes x)/2$.
+For distinct primes the squared norm of the last vector is
+$W_p(f^*\star f)W_q(h^*\star h)$.
+For $p=q$ and $f=h$, it is $2W_p(f^*\star f)^2$.
+These are the actual two-particle products, including their normalization.
+For the Gaussian, the vectors
+$e_p=v_p(g_a)/\sqrt{q_p(a)}$ are orthonormal.
+The normalized occupation vectors
+\[
+ |r,s\rangle=
+ \frac{a^*(e_2)^r a^*(e_3)^s}{\sqrt{r!s!}}\Omega
+ \qquad(r,s\geq0)
+\]
+therefore obey
+\[
+ A_2^+(g_a)|r,s\rangle
+ =\sqrt{q_2(a)}\sqrt{r+1}|r+1,s\rangle,\qquad
+ A_3^-(g_a)|r,s\rangle
+ =\sqrt{q_3(a)}\sqrt{s}|r,s-1\rangle.
+\]
+The second expression is zero when $s=0$.
+
+\subsection{The nonunital arithmetic algebra}
+
+Let $\mathcal P$ denote the set of primes.  On the disjoint union
+$X=\mathcal P\times\R$, put
+$\nu(\{p\}\times E)=\mu_p(E)$ for Borel sets $E$.
+For a finite nonempty $P\subset\mathcal P$, define
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ B_P&=\bigoplus_{p\in P}\mathscr S(\R),&
+ B_{\mathrm{loc}}&=\bigoplus_{p\in\mathcal P}^{\mathrm{alg}}\mathscr S(\R),\\
+ H_P&=\bigoplus_{p\in P}H_p,&
+ H&=\bigoplus_{p\in\mathcal P}H_p=L^2(X,\nu),\\
+ \omega(b)&=\sum_p\int_\R b_p(t)\,d\mu_p(t),&
+ (\pi(b)h)_p(t)&=b_p(t)h_p(t).
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-local-algebra}
+\end{equation}
+The superscript $\mathrm{alg}$ means finite prime support.
+Products and involution are pointwise:
+$(bd)_p=b_pd_p$ and $(b^*)_p=\overline{b_p}$.
+Give $B_{\mathrm{loc}}$ the locally convex direct sum topology of its
+Schwartz summands.  All defining integrals are finite in this algebra.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Prime algebra, weight, and arithmetic map]
+\label{prop:wc-weight}
+The algebra $B_{\mathrm{loc}}$ is nonunital, and $\omega$ is a continuous
+positive functional with zero GNS null space.
+Its GNS completion is $H$, with bounded multiplication representation $\pi$.
+For $b\in B_{\mathrm{loc}}$,
+\[
+ \|\pi(b)\|=\max_p\|b_p\|_\infty.
+\]
+The extended positive weight on $L^\infty(X,\nu)$ is
+$\Phi(b)=\int_X b\,d\nu\in[0,\infty]$ for $b\geq0$.
+Its finite ideals are
+\[
+ \mathfrak m_\Phi=L^1(\nu)\cap L^\infty(\nu),\qquad
+ \mathfrak n_\Phi=L^2(\nu)\cap L^\infty(\nu).
+\]
+One has $\Phi(1)=\infty$.
+The functional $\omega$ has no positive unitization of finite mass.
+
+For finite nonempty $P$, the map
+\begin{equation}
+ T_P:\cA_\zeta\longrightarrow B_P,\qquad
+ (T_Pf)_p=\widehat f\quad(p\in P)
+ \label{eq:wc-arithmetic-map}
+\end{equation}
+is an injective $*$-homomorphism.  With $W_P=\sum_{p\in P}W_p$,
+\[
+ \omega(T_Pg)=W_P(g),\qquad
+ \|T_Pf\|_{H_P}^2=W_P(f^*\star f).
+\]
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+An identity would restrict to the constant one in each Schwartz summand,
+which is impossible.  The density bound for $\lambda_p$ proves continuity
+of each integral in the Schwartz topology.  The direct sum topology gives
+continuity of their sum.  Positivity and the null-space assertion follow
+from
+\[
+ \omega(b^*b)=\sum_p\|b_p\|_{H_p}^2
+\]
+and $\lambda_p>0$ almost everywhere.
+Truncation in primes and density of Schwartz functions in each $H_p$
+prove density in $H$.  The multiplication norm follows by choosing a
+bump in one prime coordinate near the supremum of its multiplier.
+
+For bounded measurable functions, integration gives the stated ideals.
+The sets consisting of finitely many prime coordinates and bounded real
+intervals have finite measure and exhaust $X$.
+Their characteristic functions give finite-weight truncations of every
+nonnegative bounded function.  A single prime coordinate already has
+infinite total measure, so $\Phi(1)=\infty$.
+A finite positive unitization of $\omega$ would restrict to one for its
+$p=2$ summand, contrary to Theorem~\ref{thm:wp-carrier}.
+Finally, the Fourier product and involution identities apply in every
+coordinate.  Equation~\eqref{eq:wp-positive-realization} gives both
+functional identities and Fourier inversion gives injectivity.
+\end{proof}
+
+The minimal Hilbert space for $W_P$ alone is
+$\widetilde H_P=L^2(\R,(\sum_{p\in P}\lambda_p)dt/(2\pi))$.
+The isometry
+\[
+ \Delta_P:\widetilde H_P\longrightarrow H_P,\qquad
+ \Delta_Ph=(h)_{p\in P}
+\]
+has closed image equal to the closure of $T_P(\cA_\zeta)$.
+Density follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:wp-carrier} applied to the summable
+family for $P$.
+When $P$ contains two primes, this image is proper.
+A vector supported in just one prime coordinate cannot be diagonal
+unless it vanishes, since all $\mu_p$ are equivalent to Lebesgue measure.
+The larger space $H_P$ retains the independently addressable prime currents.
+
+\subsection{Operators and their common domain}
+
+For any of the Hilbert spaces $K=H_P$ or $K=H$, let $S_n$ be the
+orthogonal symmetrizer on the Hilbert tensor power $K^{\otimes n}$.
+Set $K^{\odot n}=S_nK^{\otimes n}$, $K^{\odot0}=\C$, and
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal F(K)=\bigoplus_{n\geq0}K^{\odot n},\qquad
+ \mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(K)
+ =\bigoplus_{n\geq0}^{\mathrm{alg}}K^{\odot n}.
+ \label{eq:wc-fock-domain}
+\end{equation}
+Each sector here is a completed Hilbert space.
+Finite particle number does not require finite prime support.
+The vacuum is $\Omega=(1,0,0,\ldots)$.
+
+For $v\in K$ and $\psi_n\in K^{\odot n}$, define
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ a^*(v)\psi_n&=\sqrt{n+1}\,S_{n+1}(v\otimes\psi_n),\\
+ a(v)\psi_n&=\sqrt n\,C_v\psi_n\quad(n\geq1),&
+ a(v)\Omega&=0.
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-creation-definition}
+\end{equation}
+The bounded contraction $C_v$ on $K^{\otimes n}$ sends
+$h_1\otimes\cdots\otimes h_n$ to
+$\langle h_1,v\rangle h_2\otimes\cdots\otimes h_n$.
+It maps symmetric tensors to symmetric tensors.
+Thus $a^*(v)$ is linear in $v$, while $a(v)$ is conjugate-linear in $v$.
+These definitions agree with the standard bosonic normalization
+\cite{wc-Derezinski}*{Sections 5.5--5.7, pp.~21--23}, after reversing
+the inner-product convention there.
+
+Regard $b\in B_P$ or $B_{\mathrm{loc}}$ as its vector $j(b)\in K$.
+Define three complex-linear families on $\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(K)$:
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ J^+(b)&=a^*(j(b)),&
+ J^-(b)&=a(j(b^*)),\\
+ \mathsf N(b)|_{K^{\odot n}}
+ &=\sum_{r=1}^n
+ I^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes\pi(b)\otimes I^{\otimes(n-r)},&
+ \mathsf N(b)\Omega&=0.
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-current-definition}
+\end{equation}
+The sum commutes with $S_n$ and therefore restricts to the displayed sector.
+The symbol $\mathsf N(b)$ denotes a number current, distinct from the
+frequency multiplier in \eqref{eq:wp-moment-domain}.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic current relations]
+\label{thm:wc-current}
+Every operator in \eqref{eq:wc-current-definition} preserves the dense
+domain $\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(K)$ and is closable.
+On this common domain, the adjoint pairing relations are
+\begin{equation}
+ J^+(b)^\dagger=J^-(b^*),\qquad
+ J^-(b)^\dagger=J^+(b^*),\qquad
+ \mathsf N(b)^\dagger=\mathsf N(b^*).
+ \label{eq:wc-current-star}
+\end{equation}
+Here $A^\dagger=B$ means
+$\langle A\psi,\eta\rangle=\langle\psi,B\eta\rangle$ on this domain.
+It does not identify the full Hilbert adjoint domain with this domain.
+All finite products are defined there, and
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ [J^-(b),J^+(d)]&=\omega(bd)I,&
+ [J^+(b),J^+(d)]&=[J^-(b),J^-(d)]=0,\\
+ [\mathsf N(b),J^+(d)]&=J^+(bd),&
+ [\mathsf N(b),J^-(d)]&=-J^-(bd),\\
+ [\mathsf N(b),\mathsf N(d)]&=0.&&
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-current-relations}
+\end{equation}
+In particular, these operators represent the associative $*$-algebra
+of finite noncommutative polynomials subject to the displayed linearity,
+involution, and commutator relations.  The generator $I$ acts as the identity.
+Creation and annihilation are unbounded for nonzero labels.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Orthogonal symmetrization and contraction give the sector bounds
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ \|a^*(v)\psi_n\|&\leq\sqrt{n+1}\|v\|\|\psi_n\|,\\
+ \|a(v)\psi_n\|&\leq\sqrt n\|v\|\|\psi_n\|,\\
+ \|\mathsf N(b)\psi_n\|&\leq n\|\pi(b)\|\|\psi_n\|.
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-sector-bounds}
+\end{equation}
+They prove invariance and finite values on every sector.
+The defining tensor inner product gives
+$\langle a^*(v)\psi,\eta\rangle=\langle\psi,a(v)\eta\rangle$.
+Likewise, the adjoint pairing for multiplication gives the one for
+$\mathsf N$.  Replacing $v$ by $j(b)$ or $j(b^*)$ proves
+\eqref{eq:wc-current-star}.
+Each densely defined operator has a densely defined operator in its
+Hilbert adjoint, and is therefore closable.
+
+On $h_1\odot\cdots\odot h_n=S_n(h_1\otimes\cdots\otimes h_n)$,
+the contraction formula is
+\[
+ a(v)(h_1\odot\cdots\odot h_n)
+ =\frac1{\sqrt n}\sum_{r=1}^n\langle h_r,v\rangle
+ h_1\odot\cdots\odot\widehat h_r\odot\cdots\odot h_n.
+\]
+Contracting a newly inserted vector gives $\langle w,v\rangle$.
+The other contractions equal those in the reverse composition.
+Hence $[a(v),a^*(w)]=\langle w,v\rangle I$.
+The creation operators commute by symmetrization, and the contraction
+operators commute by their displayed formula.
+Applying the sum defining $\mathsf N(b)$ gives
+\[
+ [\mathsf N(b),a^*(v)]=a^*(\pi(b)v),\qquad
+ [\mathsf N(b),a(v)]=-a(\pi(b^*)v).
+\]
+These equalities hold first on elementary symmetric tensors.
+The sector bounds extend each fixed product to the whole input sector,
+so they hold on $\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(K)$.
+Now
+$\langle j(d),j(b^*)\rangle=\omega(bd)$ and
+$\pi(b^*)j(d^*)=j((bd)^*)$.
+This gives every mixed relation in \eqref{eq:wc-current-relations}.
+The number currents commute because their one-particle multipliers do.
+The adjoint pairing reverses products on the invariant domain, which
+gives the stated $*$-representation.
+Finally, for $e=v/\|v\|$, the unit vector $e^{\otimes n}$ has creation
+norm $\sqrt{n+1}\|v\|$ and annihilation norm $\sqrt n\|v\|$.
+These values prove unboundedness.
+\end{proof}
+
+The currents $Q(b)=J^+(b)+J^-(b)$ and
+$P(b)=i(J^+(b)-J^-(b))$ satisfy
+\[
+ \begin{gathered}
+ Q(b)^\dagger=Q(b^*),\qquad P(b)^\dagger=P(b^*),\\
+ [Q(b),Q(d)]=[P(b),P(d)]=0,\qquad
+ [Q(b),P(d)]=2i\omega(bd)I.
+ \end{gathered}
+\]
+For a finite prime set, put
+$A_P^\pm(f)=J^\pm(T_Pf)$ and $D_P(f)=\mathsf N(T_Pf)$.
+The relations become
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ [A_P^-(f),A_P^+(h)]&=W_P(f\star h)I,\\
+ [D_P(f),A_P^\pm(h)]&=\pm A_P^\pm(f\star h),\\
+ A_P^\pm(f)^\dagger&=A_P^\mp(f^*),&
+ D_P(f)^\dagger&=D_P(f^*).
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:wc-native-current-relations}
+\end{equation}
+Moreover,
+\begin{equation}
+ \langle Q(T_Pf)\Omega,Q(T_Ph)\Omega\rangle
+ =W_P(h^*\star f).
+ \label{eq:wc-vacuum-covariance}
+\end{equation}
+This establishes the current action and its arithmetic covariance
+without changing the test algebra.
+The map $f\mapsto Q(T_Pf)$ is linear and respects the involution.
+It is not a convolution homomorphism.
+For a nonzero self-adjoint $f$, its square on $\Omega$ has nonzero
+vacuum and two-particle components, while $Q(T_P(f\star f))\Omega$
+has only a one-particle component.
+
+The central identity in the current algebra is distinct from a unit in
+$B_{\mathrm{loc}}$.  The latter still has none.
+In particular, $\langle\mathsf N(b)\Omega,\Omega\rangle=0$, even when
+$\omega(b)>0$.  Thus the vacuum is not a representing vector for
+$\omega$ under $b\mapsto\mathsf N(b)$.
+That map is also not an associative homomorphism: its two-particle
+product has cross terms absent from $\mathsf N(bd)$.
+Equation~\eqref{eq:wc-vacuum-covariance} represents the quadratic form
+through current vectors.  It supplies no finite positive unitization
+of the arithmetic weight.
+
+\subsection{Coherent prime inclusions and the infinite domain}
+
+For finite $P\subset Q$, let $e_{PQ}:B_P\to B_Q$ and
+$i_{PQ}:H_P\to H_Q$ insert zero in the new prime coordinates.
+The first is a $*$-homomorphism and preserves $\omega$.
+The second is an isometry and obeys
+$i_{PQ}\pi_P(b)=\pi_Q(e_{PQ}b)i_{PQ}$.
+Both maps compose exactly along $P\subset Q\subset R$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[The prime current system]
+\label{thm:wc-system}
+The isometries
+\[
+ \Gamma(i_{PQ})=\bigoplus_{n\geq0}i_{PQ}^{\otimes n}:
+ \mathcal F(H_P)\longrightarrow\mathcal F(H_Q)
+\]
+preserve the vacuum and intertwine $J^\pm(b)$ and $\mathsf N(b)$
+with the currents labeled by $e_{PQ}b$.
+They compose exactly.  Their Hilbert completion is $\mathcal F(H)$.
+The same operator formulas give all local currents on its common
+domain $\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(H)$.
+
+For an arbitrary vector $v=(v_p)\in H$, let $v^{(P)}$ be its finite
+prime truncation.  Then, for every $\psi\in\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(H)$,
+\begin{equation}
+ a^*(v^{(P)})\psi\longrightarrow a^*(v)\psi,\qquad
+ a(v^{(P)})\psi\longrightarrow a(v)\psi.
+ \label{eq:wc-infinite-currents}
+\end{equation}
+Conversely, convergence of $a^*(v^{(P)})\Omega$ for a prescribed family
+$(v_p)$ requires $\sum_p\|v_p\|^2<\infty$.
+Thus square summability is the exact domain for creation limits
+whose common domain contains the vacuum.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Tensor powers of an isometry commute with symmetrization and preserve
+tensor inner products.  The creation and contraction formulas therefore
+give both intertwiners.  The one-particle multiplication intertwiner
+gives the number-current identity after summing over tensor positions.
+Composition follows on each tensor power, including particle number zero.
+
+Finite prime truncations converge strongly to the identity on $H$.
+Their tensor powers converge on elementary tensors by a telescoping
+expansion, and hence on each completed tensor power by their norm bound.
+They also commute with $S_n$.
+Truncation in particle number proves density of the union of the embedded
+Fock spaces in $\mathcal F(H)$.
+
+If $\psi$ has particle number at most $N$, the sector bounds give
+\[
+ \|(a^*(v^{(P)})-a^*(v))\psi\|
+ \leq\sqrt{N+1}\|v^{(P)}-v\|\|\psi\|.
+\]
+The annihilation estimate has $\sqrt N$ in place of $\sqrt{N+1}$.
+This proves both limits.  Conversely,
+$a^*(v^{(P)})\Omega=v^{(P)}$.
+The squared distances of two such vectors are sums of squared norms
+of their differing prime coordinates.  The Cauchy criterion gives the
+necessary and sufficient square summability.
+\end{proof}
+
+The theorem yields currents with infinite prime support.
+For example, choose a unit vector $u_p\in H_p$ for every prime.
+Then $v_p=p^{-1}u_p$ has infinite support and
+$\sum_p\|v_p\|^2\leq\sum_{n\geq2}n^{-2}<\infty$.
+Its current is defined by \eqref{eq:wc-infinite-currents}.
+For labels $b,d\in H$, pointwise conjugation is still an isometry and
+$\int_X|bd|\,d\nu\leq\|b\|_H\|d\|_H$.
+Thus $J^+(b)=a^*(b)$ and $J^-(b)=a(b^*)$ extend the Heisenberg
+relations to all Hilbert labels with central pairing $\int_Xbd\,d\nu$.
+Number currents also extend to each bounded measurable multiplier $b$,
+using \eqref{eq:wc-current-definition} and
+$\|\mathsf N(b)\psi_n\|\leq n\|b\|_\infty\|\psi_n\|$.
+The two extensions have different label spaces.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Failure of the native diagonal current]
+\label{prop:wc-diagonal-barrier}
+For $f\in L^2(\R,du)$, define the formal prime family
+$\delta f=(\widehat f)_p$, using the $L^2$ Fourier transform.
+Every coordinate belongs to $H_p$.
+Its finite norm domain is exactly
+\begin{equation}
+ \{f\in L^2(\R,du):\delta f\in H\}=\{0\}.
+ \label{eq:wc-diagonal-domain}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently, for each nonzero $f\in\cA_\zeta$, neither
+$A_P^+(f)\Omega$ nor $Q(T_Pf)\Omega$ converges as $P$ increases to
+all primes.  No operators agreeing with these limits can have a common
+domain containing $\Omega$.
+For $P\subsetneq Q$, there is no isometry between the minimal
+arithmetic Hilbert spaces that sends every $T_Pf$ to $T_Qf$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The bound on $\lambda_p$ and Plancherel's identity put every coordinate
+in $H_p$.  Monotone summation gives
+\[
+ \sum_p\|\widehat f\|_{H_p}^2
+ =\sum_p\sum_{m\geq1}\frac{\log p}{p^{m/2}}
+ \|f-\tau_{m\log p}f\|_2^2.
+\]
+Theorem~\ref{thm:wp-prime-limit} proves that this equals infinity for
+every nonzero $f\in L^2$.
+All terms are nonnegative, so exhaustion by finite sets of whole prime
+factors has the same supremum as exhaustion by finite prime powers.
+This proves \eqref{eq:wc-diagonal-domain}.
+Both current vectors on the vacuum equal the finite truncation of
+$\delta f$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:wc-system} excludes their convergence.
+Finally, for nonzero $f\in\cA_\zeta$,
+\[
+ \|T_Qf\|^2-\|T_Pf\|^2
+ =\sum_{p\in Q\setminus P}W_p(f^*\star f)>0.
+\]
+An isometry preserving those vectors would require equality of the norms.
+\end{proof}
+
+The actual inclusion instead obeys
+\[
+ T_Qf=e_{PQ}T_Pf+T_{Q\setminus P}f.
+\]
+The two terms have orthogonal prime support, and their currents add.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Infinite arithmetic multiplication]
+\label{prop:wc-infinite-multiplication}
+There is an injective $*$-homomorphism
+\[
+ \rho:\cA_\zeta\longrightarrow\mathcal B(H),\qquad
+ (\rho(f)h)_p(t)=\widehat f(t)h_p(t),\qquad
+ \|\rho(f)\|=\|\widehat f\|_\infty.
+\]
+For $n\geq1$ define the bounded sector operator
+\[
+ D_{\infty,n}(f)=\sum_{r=1}^n
+ I^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes\rho(f)\otimes I^{\otimes(n-r)}
+ \quad\hbox{on }H^{\odot n},\qquad D_{\infty,0}(f)=0.
+\]
+The closed number current $D_\infty(f)$ is their direct sum, with domain
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal D(D_\infty(f))=
+ \left\{\psi\in\mathcal F(H):
+ \sum_{n\geq0}\|D_{\infty,n}(f)\psi_n\|^2<\infty\right\}.
+ \label{eq:wc-infinite-number-domain}
+\end{equation}
+Its Hilbert adjoint is $D_\infty(f^*)$, and
+$\mathcal D_{\mathrm{fin}}(H)$ is an invariant core.
+For self-adjoint $f=f^*$, the operator $D_\infty(f)$ is self-adjoint.
+On the common finite-particle domain, the prime cutoffs obey
+\[
+ D_P(f)\psi\longrightarrow D_\infty(f)\psi.
+\]
+Here $D_P(f)$ acts on $\mathcal F(H)$ by its prime-supported multiplier.
+For every $v\in H$, the same domain supports the relations
+\begin{equation}
+ [D_\infty(f),a^*(v)]=a^*(\rho(f)v),\qquad
+ [D_\infty(f),a(v)]=-a(\rho(f^*)v).
+ \label{eq:wc-infinite-mixed}
+\end{equation}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The Fourier product and involution identities prove the homomorphism
+property.  A bump in any one prime coordinate proves its norm formula.
+Fourier inversion then gives injectivity.
+Each sector operator has norm at most $n\|\widehat f\|_\infty$.
+Testing against vectors in a single sector identifies the adjoint of
+the direct sum with the direct sum of the sector adjoints.
+These are $D_{\infty,n}(f^*)$.
+The maximal direct sum domain in \eqref{eq:wc-infinite-number-domain}
+is closed: convergence of a vector and its image gives the required
+identity in each sector, and the limiting image is square summable.
+Particle truncation approximates both a vector and its image, which
+proves the core assertion.  For $f=f^*$ the adjoint has the same domain
+and action, proving self-adjointness.
+
+Prime truncations of $\rho(f)$ converge strongly on $H$, with a uniform
+bound $\|\widehat f\|_\infty$.
+The tensor-position sums therefore converge strongly on each sector.
+There are only finitely many sectors in $\psi$, giving the claimed limit.
+The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:wc-current} for an arbitrary bounded
+one-particle multiplier gives \eqref{eq:wc-infinite-mixed}.
+\end{proof}
+
+Thus the native diagonal defines a number current for every arithmetic
+test.  Its creation vector exists only for the zero test, by
+Proposition~\ref{prop:wc-diagonal-barrier}.
+
+\subsection{The full Weil comparison}
+
+Let $\mathcal R(g)$ be the sum of the two polar evaluations and the
+archimedean integral in \eqref{eq:weil-functional}, and set
+$C_P=\sum_{p\in P}C_p$.  For every $g\in\cA_\zeta$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ L_\zeta(g)-W_P(g)
+ ={}&\mathcal R(g)-2C_Pg(0)\\
+ &-\sum_{p\notin P}\sum_{m\geq1}
+ \frac{\log p}{p^{m/2}}
+ \bigl(g(m\log p)+g(-m\log p)\bigr).
+ \end{split}
+ \label{eq:wc-weil-comparison}
+\end{equation}
+The double tail tends to zero along increasing finite prime sets.
+Indeed, the native prime series converges absolutely on this test algebra,
+so dominated convergence permits regrouping and exhaustion by primes.
+Also $C_P\to\infty$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:wp-prime-limit}.
+For a fixed nonzero square $g=f^*\star f$, the residual in
+\eqref{eq:wc-weil-comparison} therefore tends to $-\infty$.
+
+In particular, an additional orthogonal positive current sector cannot
+turn these cumulative currents into a covariance for $L_\zeta$.
+Such a sector would require
+$L_\zeta(f^*\star f)-W_P(f^*\star f)\geq0$ for every test $f$.
+The preceding limit violates that condition for every fixed nonzero
+$f$ once $P$ is sufficiently large.
+A positive realization of the full functional must instead combine
+the prime, polar, and archimedean terms before assigning a Hilbert norm.
+It requires an actual map $V$ on the same test algebra with
+\[
+ \langle V(f),V(h)\rangle=L_\zeta(h^*\star f),
+\]
+together with the desired operator products and their common domain.
+The present currents establish that identity with $W_P$ in place of
+$L_\zeta$.  Their commutation relations do not provide the displayed
+full-functional identity or a comparison with other quantum current algebras.
